KNOWNBUG
deduced.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ deduced parameter: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.deduct.call]/2-3: for P = T & the argument type is the
function type, so T = void(int).  The front end deduces from the decayed
pointer and finds no match for call_deduced.  Deducing from the function
type (in deduce_from_call_arguments) fixes this test but then a
forwarding reference deduces F = int (&)(int, int), and
cpp11_function_type_arg_pack_pattern's decay<F> selects the partial
specialization decay<T &> instead of the more specialized
decay<R (&)(A...)>: the front end orders `T &' against a pattern with a
function parameter pack wrongly (with `R (&)(A1, A2)' it is right).
Pre-existing (bin53).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program
(-std=c++98), and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
